## TL;DR — 60-Second Answer

**Marquise diamond resale: the two numbers that define your outcome:**

1. **Natural marquise diamonds** recover 40–50% of original retail price at resale — a 50–60% loss on what you paid
2. **Lab marquise diamonds** recover 10–20% of original retail price at resale — an 80–90% loss on what you paid

**Why both numbers are real:**

- Natural diamonds have a functioning secondary market: dealers, estate buyers, auction houses
- Lab diamonds have unlimited supply; new retail keeps falling; resale bids follow it down
- Marquise is a fancy shape — it gets a 7–12 point additional discount vs round brilliant at resale
- A GIA certificate recovers 20–30% more than an uncertified stone of identical quality

**The one thing to know:** If you paid retail for a natural [marquise diamond](/marquise-cut-diamond), expect 40–50 cents back on the dollar. If you paid retail for a lab marquise, expect 10–20 cents. Which number applies to you was decided when you made the purchase.

---

[Marquise cut diamonds](/marquise-cut-diamond) surprise most sellers at resale. The surprise isn't that diamonds lose value. Most buyers know that going in. The surprise is how large the gap is — between natural and lab, and between marquise and round.

A natural marquise sold six months after purchase returns 40–50% of retail. A lab marquise sold at the same point returns 10–20%. Same shape. Completely different resale landscape.

This guide covers both numbers, what drives them, and specific steps to recover more. Full range: 0.5ct natural to 2ct lab, pawn shops to auction houses.

## What Makes Marquise Diamond Resale Different

The [marquise cut](/marquise-cut-diamond) is an elongated fancy shape. The resale market for fancy shapes works differently from round brilliant.

Round brilliant is the universal diamond. Every buyer knows it. Every dealer prices it. Auction houses sell it every week. The secondary market is deep, liquid, and consistent.

Marquise is not universal. Buyers self-select into it. They chose the elongated shape. The finger-slimming effect. The vintage character. The larger face-up area per carat. Not every buyer is looking for marquise. That narrower audience means lower secondary market demand. And lower demand means lower resale bids.

The shape discount at resale is real. A 1ct G VS1 round brilliant recovers more per carat than a 1ct G VS1 marquise. The marquise buyer pool is smaller. Dealers price that risk.

This is the baseline for everything that follows. Natural marquise starts from a slight disadvantage vs round. Lab marquise compounds that disadvantage dramatically.

## The Fancy Shape Tax

The Fancy Shape Tax is the resale penalty marquise pays vs round brilliant at the same carat weight.

In the primary market, marquise sells for 10–30% less per carat than round at equivalent grades. That discount reflects lower cutting prestige and narrower buyer demand.

At resale, the discount grows. Dealers managing inventory risk know a round brilliant at 1ct G VS1 sells in days. A marquise at the same specs may sit for weeks. That extra hold time is priced into the buy offer.

**The Fancy Shape Tax operates on three levels:**

1. **Fewer bidders** — fewer buyers targeting marquise means less competitive pressure on offers
2. **Longer hold times** — dealers holding marquise longer discount their bids accordingly
3. **Shape fashion risk** — marquise popularity cycles; dealers price in that uncertainty

The Fancy Shape Tax adds 7–12 percentage points of additional discount vs round at resale.

For a natural 1ct G VS1 marquise, a dealer might offer 35–45% of retail. The same quality round brilliant gets 45–55%. That 10-point gap is the Fancy Shape Tax.

For lab marquise, the Fancy Shape Tax compounds on an already-collapsed floor. The absolute dollar difference between a lab round and lab marquise is smaller — because both are near-zero.

## Natural vs Lab: The 50% vs 10% Split

This is the central divide in [marquise diamond](/marquise-cut-diamond) resale. The gap between natural and lab is larger than most buyers realize.

### Natural Marquise Diamond Resale: The 40–50% Number

Natural marquise diamonds have a real secondary market. Estate jewelers buy them. Diamond dealers buy them. Auction houses list them. This competition for inventory creates a functioning price floor.

That floor is anchored by the Rapaport Price List. This is the wholesale price guide used by the trade. Dealers bid as a percentage of Rap. For marquise, dealer bids fall at 30–50% of retail.

**What gets you closer to 50%:**
- GIA or AGS certificate
- 1ct or larger
- G-H color, VS1-VS2 clarity
- L/W ratio of 1.85:1 to 2.00:1
- No bowtie effect
- Perfect, unchipped tips

**What drops you closer to 30%:**
- No certificate or IGI-only cert
- Under 0.75ct
- Visible bowtie
- Extreme L/W ratio
- Chipped tips

### Lab Marquise Diamond Resale: The 10–20% Number

Lab diamonds have fundamentally undermined their own resale market through supply dynamics.

When you bought a lab marquise, you paid retail. That retail price was already 40–70% below natural equivalent — see our [lab-grown marquise diamond guide](/lab-grown-marquise-diamond) for the full purchase case. The resale buyer knows this. Current lab retail is often less than what you paid. They have no incentive to pay near your purchase price.

Lab diamond retail prices have been falling 10–30% per year in many carat ranges. Consider what that means:

- A 1ct lab marquise you bought 18 months ago for $2,000 may cost $1,400 new today
- A dealer offers below current new retail
- The resale offer might be $200–400 on a $2,000 purchase

That's 10–20% back. It is not a defect in the stone. It is how lab diamond economics work. Unlimited supply, no scarcity, perpetually falling prices.

[Lab-grown marquise diamonds](/lab-grown-marquise-diamond) are excellent for wear. They are poor for resale. Know that going in.

> **"I see clients every week who are shocked by their lab diamond resale quotes. They paid $3,000 for a 1.5ct lab marquise two years ago. A dealer offers them $400. The stone is beautiful. The offer is real. Lab diamonds don't have a secondary market the way natural diamonds do. That's the trade-off. The initial savings were real. The resale gap is also real."**
> — Farzana Hasan, diamond expert | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

**Verdict:** Natural marquise: 40–50% of retail. Lab marquise: 10–20% of retail. Buy lab for wear and savings. Buy natural if you think you might sell.

## Resale Recovery by Carat, Cert, and Stone Type

Resale percentages don't scale linearly with carat weight. Larger, certified stones recover better percentages than smaller, uncertified ones.

| Stone | Carat | Certificate | Resale Recovery | Estimated Range | What this means |
|-------|-------|-------------|-----------------|-----------------|-----------------|
| Natural marquise | 0.5ct | GIA | 35–42% | $150–$700 | Under 1ct has thin demand — few private buyers compete |
| Natural marquise | 1ct | GIA | 40–50% | $1,000–$2,500 | Most liquid size; dealers and private buyers both active |
| Natural marquise | 2ct | GIA | 45–55% | $3,500–$8,000 | Larger stones recover better percentages via auction |
| Natural marquise | 1ct | None | 25–35% | $600–$1,400 | No cert = dealer prices in the risk of re-grading |
| Lab marquise | 1ct | IGI | 10–20% | $150–$400 | Current new lab retail may be below your purchase price |
| Lab marquise | 2ct | IGI | 8–15% | $250–$600 | Same economics; more dollar pain at larger investment |

> **"Certificates matter more at resale than at purchase. A buyer falls in love with the stone. A resale dealer buys the paper. A GIA certificate removes re-grading risk. That translates directly into a higher buy offer. I've seen 1ct natural marquise stones with GIA certs get $400 more than identical-looking uncertified stones. Every time."**
> — Farzana Hasan, diamond expert | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

**Verdict:** Natural, GIA-certified, 1ct or larger is the strongest resale profile. Lab, any size, any cert — plan on 10–20% back.

## The Lab Floor

The Lab Floor is the price floor below which lab diamond resale cannot meaningfully fall. It is already low enough to hurt.

The floor exists because of friction, not demand. A 1ct lab marquise will always find a buyer at some price. In practice, that price sits at 10–20% of original retail. Three things set that floor:

1. **Scrap metal value** — the setting itself has melt value; buyers factor that in
2. **Uninformed private buyers** — some private buyers don't know lab from natural
3. **Jewelry resellers** — some resellers buy lab pieces to resell as pre-owned estate

The Lab Floor doesn't rise over time. It stays low or falls. Lab retail prices keep declining as production efficiency improves. The resale floor follows.

The implication is clear. A [lab marquise](/lab-grown-marquise-diamond) is a beautiful stone for daily wear. It is not a store of value. Do not calculate resale as part of a lab diamond purchase decision. Buy it for the shape, the savings, and the enjoyment. Leave resale out of the math.

## How to Maximize Marquise Resale Value

If you plan to sell, these four levers are within your control.

### Get the Certificate Right

The [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) report is the resale industry standard for natural diamonds. An uncertified natural marquise trades at a significant discount. Submit for a GIA report before selling. The cost is $100–150 for stones under 2ct. That fee typically returns $300–600 in a higher buy offer.

For lab diamonds, the [IGI](https://www.igi.org/) report is the most widely accepted by resale dealers. GIA also grades lab stones. Either improves your offer — but the absolute dollar gain is smaller given the already-low lab floor.

### Sell Private Before Selling to a Dealer

A dealer needs margin. They buy at 30–50% to resell at 60–80%. A private buyer doesn't need that margin.

Platforms where private sales recover more:
- Worthy.com (online auction, competitive dealer bidding)
- I Do Now I Don't (dedicated jewelry resale marketplace)
- Facebook Marketplace (works for lower-carat stones, local buyers)
- Esty Collective (curated estate jewelry buyers)

Private sale returns 10–20 percentage points more than dealer sale. The trade-off is time and effort.

### Protect the Tips

Chipped tips on a [marquise diamond ring](/marquise-diamond-ring) reduce resale value significantly. The pointed ends are the most vulnerable points of the stone. A chip requires re-polishing, which reduces carat weight and changes the grade. A perfect-tip stone commands 15–25% more than a chipped one at resale.

If you own a marquise in a prong setting, inspect the tips regularly. A [bezel setting](/bezel-setting-diamond) eliminates tip exposure entirely. For [marquise engagement rings](/marquise-diamond-engagement-ring), V-prongs at both tips reduce chip risk versus a basket or martini arrangement.

### Keep the Paperwork

Original purchase receipts, insurance appraisals, and the GIA certificate together form a provenance package. Buyers and dealers pay more when they can verify history. An appraisal on file shows insurable value. A receipt shows original purchase context. Neither document is required — but both improve buyer confidence and offer amounts.

For inherited or gifted marquise diamonds, ask for the GIA certificate before transferring ownership. Without it, the new owner must submit for grading before resale. That extra cost and delay reduces final recovery.

### Time the Market for Natural Stones

Natural diamond prices move in 10–20% cycles over multi-year periods. Selling at a market high recovers more than selling in a trough. Lab diamond prices only move down — sell sooner, not later.

## Where to Sell a Marquise Diamond

Different channels produce meaningfully different results.

| Channel | Natural Recovery | Lab Recovery | Speed | What this means |
|---------|-----------------|-------------|-------|-----------------|
| Local jeweler / dealer | 25–40% | 5–15% | Immediate | Fastest exit; lowest price; convenience is priced in |
| Online auction (Worthy.com) | 38–55% | 10–20% | 2–4 weeks | Competitive bidding lifts natural offers significantly |
| Private sale (I Do Now I Don't) | 42–60% | 12–25% | 2–8 weeks | Best recovery; most effort; requires buyer trust |
| Auction house (Heritage, Sotheby's) | 48–65% for 2ct+ | Rarely accepts lab | 4–8 weeks | Worthwhile for large natural with strong provenance |
| Pawn shop | 20–30% | 5–10% | Immediate | Fastest; worst recovery; last resort only |

The channel matters as much as the stone quality. A 1ct natural GIA marquise at a pawn shop returns $500. The same stone through Worthy.com competitive bidding may return $1,200–$1,800.

For [marquise diamond engagement rings](/marquise-diamond-engagement-ring), the setting also holds value. A platinum or 18k gold setting adds $200–$600 to total recovery. Some buyers want the complete piece. Others want the loose stone. List both options to compare.

## Marquise vs Other Fancy Shapes at Resale

Marquise is not the only fancy shape that carries a resale discount. Oval, pear, and cushion all trade below round brilliant at resale. But they don't all trade at the same discount.

**Oval** is the most popular fancy shape by buyer volume. That larger buyer pool means more competition at resale — and higher bids. Oval natural diamonds often recover 40–55% of retail at resale. They outperform marquise by 5–10 percentage points at the same quality tier.

**Pear** is similar to [marquise](/marquise-cut-diamond) in buyer depth — both are elongated shapes with pointed tips and a narrower secondary market. Pear and marquise tend to recover within 3–5 percentage points of each other.

**Cushion** has the broadest fancy shape buyer pool after oval. It recovers 38–52% at resale. Close to marquise but with slightly more buyer demand.

**Princess** has fallen out of fashion. Despite once being the most popular fancy shape, resale demand has softened. Recovery is 30–45% — often worse than marquise for stones cut before 2015 when princess was more prevalent in the market.

| Fancy Shape | Natural Resale Recovery | vs Marquise | What this means |
|-------------|------------------------|-------------|-----------------|
| Oval | 40–55% | +5–10 pts | Most popular fancy shape; widest buyer pool |
| Cushion | 38–52% | +3–7 pts | Second-widest buyer pool among fancy shapes |
| Marquise | 38–50% | Baseline | Strong shape identity; narrower buyer audience |
| Pear | 35–50% | ±3 pts | Elongated with tip; similar resale dynamics to marquise |
| Princess | 30–45% | −5–8 pts | Fell out of fashion; thinner secondary market |

**Verdict:** Among fancy shapes, marquise is mid-pack at resale. It outperforms princess. It underperforms oval and cushion. If you love marquise and are choosing between marquise and oval for resale reasons alone, oval recovers slightly more. But the 5–10 percentage point gap on a $3,000 purchase is $150–300 in absolute terms. Buy the shape you want.

## Round vs Marquise at Resale: The Baseline

Buyers comparing a [marquise cut](/marquise-cut-diamond) to a round brilliant often ask about resale directly. Here is the honest comparison.

| Quality | Round Brilliant Natural | Marquise Natural | Shape Penalty | What this means |
|---------|------------------------|-----------------|---------------|-----------------|
| 1ct G VS1, GIA | 45–60% of retail | 38–50% of retail | 7–12 pts | Round recovers more per carat; deeper buyer pool |
| 1ct H SI1, GIA | 40–52% of retail | 33–44% of retail | 7–10 pts | Gap is consistent across color/clarity tiers |
| 2ct G VS2, GIA | 50–65% of retail | 42–55% of retail | 8–12 pts | Both recover better at 2ct; gap persists |
| 1ct G VS1 lab | 10–20% of retail | 8–18% of retail | 2–5 pts | Lab gap between shapes is smaller; both near the floor |

**Verdict:** Round brilliant outperforms marquise at resale by 7–12 percentage points. If resale is your primary criterion, round brilliant is the correct choice. If you want the marquise shape — the elongating visual effect, the larger face-up area, the vintage character — that gap is the cost of choosing the shape you actually want. Most buyers who want marquise don't regret it.

## Certificates and the Resale Premium

A GIA certificate adds measurable value at resale. The premium is consistent across marquise size and quality tiers.

Dealers discount uncertified stones because they absorb the risk of the true grade being lower than claimed. That risk costs them money. An uncertified 1ct that looks like G VS1 might bid at 28–35% of retail. A GIA-certified 1ct at the same grade bids at 40–50%.

The certificate premium at resale:
- 0.5–0.75ct natural marquise: +$200–400 with GIA vs uncertified
- 1ct natural marquise: +$300–600 with GIA vs uncertified
- 2ct natural marquise: +$800–1,500 with GIA vs uncertified

The rule is simple: never send a natural marquise diamond to resale without a GIA report. The certification fee always pays for itself — every time.

## The Bowtie Factor at Resale

[Marquise diamonds](/marquise-cut-diamond) with a visible bowtie are discounted at resale beyond the standard fancy shape penalty.

A bowtie is the dark shadow across the center width of a poorly cut marquise. Mild bowties are acceptable. Severe bowties make the center of the stone look dark and dead. Resale dealers spot bowties immediately. They price them.

A severe bowtie adds 5–15% additional discount on top of the base resale figure. A mild or invisible bowtie carries no penalty.

The ideal L/W ratio for minimal bowtie risk is 1.85:1 to 2.00:1. The full [marquise cut diamond guide](/marquise-cut-diamond) covers how to read bowtie severity from a certificate. Stones outside this range face buyer resistance. Very narrow (2.2:1+) or very wide (1.6:1 or less) ratios are hard to place. Demand for extreme ratios is thin.

If your marquise has a severe bowtie, expect offers below the averages in this guide. Factor that in before setting price expectations.

## Marquise Diamond Resale vs Other Jewelry Pieces

Selling a [marquise necklace](/marquise-diamond-necklace), [marquise earrings](/marquise-diamond-earrings), or [marquise wedding band](/marquise-diamond-wedding-band) involves the same stone value calculation — plus the setting.

Setting metal values:
- 14k white or yellow gold: $25–50 per gram melt; a typical ring is 3–5 grams
- Platinum: $35–65 per gram melt; heavier settings add meaningful scrap value
- Silver: minimal value; not a resale factor

For [marquise diamond earrings](/marquise-diamond-earrings): CTW is split between two stones. Two 0.5ct stones together resell for less than one 1ct stone at the same combined quality. Smaller per-ear stones (0.25ct each in a 0.5 CTW pair) have the thinnest resale demand.

For [marquise diamond necklaces](/marquise-diamond-necklace): pendants typically hold a single stone. The 0.5–1ct range has decent resale demand if GIA certified. Sub-0.25ct pendants resell for near-scrap value.

For [marquise engagement rings](/marquise-diamond-engagement-ring): the complete piece often sells better than a loose stone in private channels. Buyers are purchasing the narrative alongside the gem. List the complete piece and the loose stone separately to compare which attracts more interest.

## What to Expect at a Dealer Appointment

Most sellers walk into a dealer appointment without a reference point. They leave with a number they don't know how to evaluate. Here is what actually happens.

**Step 1: Visual inspection.** The dealer looks at both tips first on a marquise. Then the bowtie. Then the girdle condition. Any chip, crack, or abrasion gets noted immediately. This inspection takes 2–3 minutes.

**Step 2: Certificate review.** If you have a GIA report, the dealer reads the grading details and cross-checks them against the stone. No cert means they have to estimate — and they price that risk conservatively.

**Step 3: Market price check.** Dealers use Rapaport pricing or a live market reference. They calculate what the stone would sell for to another buyer, then back out their margin. That margin is typically 20–40% of the buy offer.

**Step 4: The offer.** The first offer is not always the final offer. You can ask "what's your best number?" and "what would improve this offer?" Good dealers will tell you. If the tips were perfect and you had a cert, the offer goes up. If not, it doesn't.

**What to bring:**
- GIA certificate (or IGI for lab stones)
- Original purchase receipt if available
- Any insurance appraisal on file
- Photos of the stone in setting (helps for provenance context)

**What not to say:** Don't tell the dealer what you paid. The offer is based on current market, not your purchase price. If you anchor on what you paid, the conversation goes sideways.

Get at least three dealer quotes before accepting any offer. The spread between the lowest and highest offer for the same [marquise diamond](/marquise-cut-diamond) is often 20–30%. Always get multiple quotes.

