## Expert Summary

**By Farzana Hasan, diamond expert** | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

Marquise diamond resale comes down to one distinction: natural or lab.

Natural marquise diamonds retain 40–50% of retail value. The secondary market is real. GIA certification, 1ct or above, G-H color, and VS1-VS2 clarity move you toward 50% rather than 40%.

Lab marquise diamonds retain 10–20% of retail value. This is not a product flaw. It reflects unlimited supply and falling retail prices. Lab diamonds offer real purchase savings. They offer near-zero resale returns.

The Fancy Shape Tax adds 7–12 percentage points of discount below round brilliant for natural marquise. It is consistent across carat weights. If resale value is your primary concern, natural round brilliant is the correct choice. If the marquise shape is what you want, buy it, wear it, and understand the math going in.

**Pros at resale (natural marquise):**
- Functioning secondary market through dealers, auctions, and private buyers
- GIA certification substantially lifts offers
- 2ct+ stones recover 45–55% through competitive channels
- Setting adds scrap metal value on top of stone recovery

**Cons at resale (all marquise):**
- Fancy shape penalty: 7–12 points below round brilliant
- Bowtie stones receive additional discount
- Extreme L/W ratios face buyer resistance
- Lab marquise: near-zero resale regardless of quality or cert

## Final Verdict

**Best-case natural resale:** 1ct+, GIA-certified, G-H color, VS1-VS2, perfect tips, L/W ratio 1.85–2.00:1, sold through Worthy.com or private sale. Recovery: 48–58% of retail.

**Worst-case natural resale:** Under 0.75ct, uncertified, visible bowtie, chipped tips, sold to a pawn shop. Recovery: 20–28% of retail.

**Best-case lab resale:** 1ct+, IGI-certified, sold quickly before retail prices fall further. Recovery: 15–25% of retail.

**Worst-case lab resale:** 2ct+ lab marquise purchased 24+ months ago with current retail below your purchase price. Dealer offer: 5–12% of original retail.

| Scenario | Stone | Channel | Expected Recovery | What this means |
|---------|-------|---------|-------------------|-----------------|
| Best natural | 1ct GIA marquise, perfect tips | Online auction (Worthy) | 48–58% | Certificate + competitive bids = best outcome |
| Average natural | 1ct GIA marquise | Local jeweler | 38–48% | Typical; fastest exit; 10-15 pts below private sale |
| Worst natural | 0.5ct uncertified, bowtie | Pawn shop | 20–28% | Get GIA cert first — it always pays for itself |
| Best lab | 1ct IGI lab marquise | Private sale | 15–25% | Sell quickly; lab prices keep falling each year |
| Worst lab | 2ct lab purchased 2+ yrs ago | Any dealer | 5–12% | Offer priced against current new lab retail, not yours |

**Verdict:** Know your stone type before you sell. Natural marquise: plan for 40–50% back with the right certificate and channel. Lab marquise: plan for 10–20% back regardless of channel. The 50% vs 10% gap in this guide's title is real. Which number applies to you was decided at purchase.

